# Target C: eight-no-root `k=7` containment and capacity reduction

Date: 2026-09-21

Status: rigorous structural reduction of the rank-one eight-no-root layer. Six of the nine arithmetic boundary cases are excluded. Three arithmetic branches remain, with their virtual-canonical profiles and deletion shadows sharply constrained.

Companion arithmetic checker:

- `research/verify_target_c_support7_global_segment_relative_eight_noroot_k7_reduction_2026_09_21.py`

## 1. Setup

Let `B=R union E`, `|E|=8`, be a hypothetical eight-no-root counterexample and write

`D(X)=|X|-3 delta(X)`.

The established seven-no-root theorem implies that every external row is an `M`-coloop and a `Q`-noncoloop. Hence

`D(B) in {-2,-1}`.

This note treats `k=7`. Thus the external `Q`-quotient rank is one. Let

`P <= Q^8`

be the corresponding coefficient line. Because no external row is a `Q`-coloop, a generator of `P` has support at least two.

For a coordinate `i`, call its one-row deletion good when the projected line `P_i` has support at least two. If `P` has support exactly two, precisely the six zero coordinates are good. If `P` has support at least three, all eight coordinates are good. Thus there are always at least six good deletions.

The exact root-block formula is

`D(B)=8+p+3d_root-3k = p+3d_root-13`.

The arithmetic boundary is therefore

```text
D=-2: (d_root,p)=(0,11),(1,8),(2,5),(3,2)
D=-1: (d_root,p)=(0,12),(1,9),(2,6),(3,3),(4,0).
```

## 2. Defect-free seven-row rank-one containment lemma

The containment argument in the committed seven-no-root `k=6` reduction is structural; its proof does not use a negative seven-row defect once the coefficient line has support at least two.

Concretely, for any seven-row rank-one coefficient line `L<=Q^7` with support at least two, define its virtual-canonical complete set `C(L)` exactly as in the preceding work. The six-row rank-one containment theorem applied to the good coordinate deletions gives

```text
|C(L)| - rank H(C(L)) >= 2,
external null(L) relations subset H(C(L)).
```

For completeness, the spanning point is the same as before. If `N=null(L)` and

`N_j={alpha in N: alpha_j=0}`,

then there are at least two good coordinate hyperplanes `N_j`, and two distinct such hyperplanes span `N`. The six-row relations lift back without correction because a block omitted from a deletion complete set is either untouched, in which case `alpha_j=0`, or deletion-virtual-empty, in which case its projected quotient demand vanishes.

This seven-row lemma can therefore be used as an input for another deletion lift.

## 3. Lift containment to eight rows

Define the eight-row virtual-canonical complete set `C` by declaring a touched root block complete exactly when its empty-state coefficient-constraint space is not contained in `P`. Put

`d_v=|C|-rank H(C)`.

For a good coordinate `i`, let `C_i` be the seven-row virtual-complete set of the deletion shadow. Projection of a globally virtual-empty block is still virtual-empty or untouched, so

`C_i subset C`.

The seven-row lemma gives

`d_i>=2`,

hence

`d_v>=2`.

Let `N=null(P)` and, for good `i`,

`N_i={alpha in N: alpha_i=0}`.

At least two good `N_i` are distinct. If `support(P)=2`, choose two zero coordinates. If `support(P)>=3`, equality of two coordinate hyperplanes would put a nonzero vector supported on those two coordinates in the line `P`, impossible.

Distinct codimension-one hyperplanes in `N` span `N`. For `alpha in N_i`, the seven-row deletion relation lies in `H(C_i)`. The same lift used in the previous rank-one argument shows the eight-row relation lies in `H(C_i) subset H(C)`. Therefore

`external null(P) relations subset H(C)`.

Let `q` be the number of virtual-complete blocks made incomplete in the actual state. A virtual-complete block cannot be actual-empty, so `q<=p`. The standard containment accounting now gives

`D(B) >= 8+p+3d_v-3q`.

Using `d_v>=2` and `q<=p`,

`D(B) >= 14-2p`.

## 4. Five arithmetic cases close immediately

The crude bound excludes

```text
D=-2: (d_root,p)=(2,5),(3,2)
D=-1: (d_root,p)=(2,6),(3,3),(4,0).
```

The only arithmetic cases left at this point are

```text
A: D=-2, d_root=0, p=11
B: D=-2, d_root=1, p=8
C: D=-1, d_root=0, p=12
D: D=-1, d_root=1, p=9.
```

The global containment inequality gives the following possible `(d_v,q,s_min)` triples, where

`s_min=max(0,2q-p)`

is the minimum number of singleton blocks among the `q` virtual-complete incomplete blocks:

```text
A, p=11:
(2,9,7),(2,10,9),(2,11,11),(3,10,9),(3,11,11),(4,11,11)

B, p=8:
(2,8,8)

C, p=12:
(2,9,6),(2,10,8),(2,11,10),(2,12,12),
(3,10,8),(3,11,10),(3,12,12),
(4,11,10),(4,12,12),(5,12,12)

D, p=9:
(2,8,7),(2,9,9),(3,9,9).
```

## 5. Projection-loss lemma for singleton capacity

A second lift turns the exact six-row deficiency-two capacity bound into seven- and eight-row bounds.

Fix a rank-one virtual-canonical state and a virtual-complete block `b` that admits a compatible singleton state.

For a good coordinate deletion `i`, the block fails to remain a singleton-capable virtual-complete block for only two possible reasons.

1. `b` becomes untouched. This can happen for at most one coordinate, because then its full external touch support is exactly `{i}`.
2. `b` remains touched but becomes virtual-empty. If `E_b` is the empty-state coefficient-constraint space, this means

   `pi_i(E_b) subset pi_i(P)`,

   equivalently

   `E_b subset P + span(e_i)`.

   This can happen for at most one good coordinate. For two distinct good coordinates `i,j`, the intersection

   `(P+span(e_i)) intersect (P+span(e_j))`

   equals `P`: in the support-two case the good coordinates lie outside `support(P)`, and in the support-at-least-three case `support(P)` is not contained in `{i,j}`. Two such collapses would therefore imply `E_b subset P`, contradicting that `b` is globally virtual-complete.

A globally singleton-capable complete block is therefore counted in all but at most two good deletion capacities.

## 6. Lift the exact deficiency-two singleton bound

The committed exact six-row all-touch audit proves:

> every synchronized six-row rank-one virtual state of deficiency two has singleton capacity at most three.

Consider a seven-row rank-one virtual state of deficiency two.

- If its coefficient line has support two, there are five good deletions. Every good deletion has deficiency exactly two, so if the seven-row singleton capacity is `S`,

  `S(5-2) <= 5*3`,

  hence `S<=5`.

- If its coefficient line has support at least three, all seven deletions are good, so

  `S(7-2) <= 7*3`,

  hence `S<=4`.

Thus

```text
seven-row d=2 singleton capacity:
support 2     => at most 5
support >=3   => at most 4.
```

Apply the projection-loss lemma once more to an eight-row deficiency-two state.

- If `support(P)=2`, all six good seven-row deletions still have support two and capacity at most five. Hence

  `S(6-2)<=6*5`,

  so `S<=7`.

- If `support(P)=3`, five zero-coordinate deletions have seven-row support three and capacity at most four, while three support-coordinate deletions have support two and capacity at most five. Thus

  `S(8-2)<=5*4+3*5=35`,

  so `S<=5`.

- If `support(P)>=4`, every seven-row deletion has support at least three, so

  `S(8-2)<=8*4`,

  again giving `S<=5`.

Therefore

```text
eight-row d_v=2 singleton capacity:
support 2     => at most 7
support >=3   => at most 5.
```

In particular, any eight-row deficiency-two state with singleton capacity at least six has coefficient support exactly two.

## 7. Close branch B completely

Branch B has

`D=-2,p=8`.

The global containment inequality has only one possibility:

`d_v=2,q=8`.

All eight changed virtual-complete blocks must therefore be singletons. This requires singleton capacity at least eight, contradicting the eight-row deficiency-two maximum of seven.

Hence

> `D=-2,k=7,d_root=1,p=8` is impossible.

Six of the nine arithmetic `k=7` cases are now closed.

## 8. Remove the remaining deficiency-two profiles in A and D

For a one-row deletion, the seven-row shadow has `k=6` and defect

```text
D_7=0 in the D=-2 branch,
D_7=1 in the D=-1 branch.
```

Its incomplete-row count differs from the global `p` by a multiple of three. A selected row contributes to the one-row loss for at most one coordinate, so the sum of all eight one-row losses is at most `p`.

Consequently the four original residuals have at least the following numbers of good deletions that retain `p` exactly:

```text
p=11: at least 3
p=8:  at least 4
p=12: at least 2
p=9:  at least 3.
```

Now consider branch A with global `(d_v,q)=(2,9)`. The eight-row capacity bound forces coefficient support two and exactly seven actual singleton blocks among the nine changed blocks; the other two are pairs, consuming all `p=11` rows.

Choose a good deletion with `p_i=11`. Then `d_i=2`. Seven-row containment gives `q_i>=8`. Any eight of the global seven singleton plus two pair blocks contain at least six singleton blocks. But a seven-row deficiency-two state has singleton capacity at most five. Contradiction.

Thus all `d_v=2` profiles in A are impossible; the `q=10,11` profiles were already excluded by the global capacity-seven bound.

Similarly, in branch D the profile `(d_v,q)=(2,8)` forces exactly seven singleton blocks and one pair. A good unchanged deletion has `d_i=2` and seven-row containment gives `q_i>=7`. Any seven of those eight blocks contain at least six singletons, again contradicting the seven-row capacity-five bound. The `(2,9)` profile is already globally impossible because it requires nine singleton blocks.

Therefore branch D is forced to

`d_v=3,q=9`,

and all nine changed blocks are singletons.

## 9. The special deficiency-two residual in branch C

For branch C, the global deficiency-two possibilities with `q>=10` require at least eight singleton blocks and are impossible by the eight-row capacity-seven bound. Only

`d_v=2,q=9`

can remain at deficiency two.

This profile has at least six actual singleton blocks among the nine changed blocks. The global capacity bound forces coefficient support exactly two.

If there were seven or more actual singleton blocks, a good unchanged deletion would have `d_i=2`, `q_i>=8`, and any eight of the nine changed blocks would contain at least six singletons, contradicting seven-row capacity five.

Hence this profile is exact:

```text
six singleton blocks,
three pair blocks,
no additional selected incomplete rows.
```

Moreover, every good deletion retaining `p_i=12` must have

```text
d_i=2,
q_i=8,
exactly five of the six singleton blocks,
all three pair blocks.
```

There are at least two such good unchanged deletions. This is a small, rigid support-two subproblem.

## 10. Remaining `k=7` target

After the reductions above, only three arithmetic branches remain:

```text
A: D=-2, d_root=0, p=11
C: D=-1, d_root=0, p=12
D: D=-1, d_root=1, p=9.
```

Their surviving virtual profiles are

```text
A:
(d_v,q,s_min)=(3,10,9),(3,11,11),(4,11,11)

C:
(2,9,6),
(3,10,8),(3,11,10),(3,12,12),
(4,11,10),(4,12,12),(5,12,12)

D:
(3,9,9).
```

The unchanged-shadow budget gives at least three, two, and three good `p_i=p` seven-row shadows for A, C, and D respectively.

For A and D, every such shadow has deficiency at least three. In the smallest A profile `(d_v,q)=(3,10)`, an unchanged shadow must have `d_i=3`, `q_i>=9`, and singleton capacity at least eight. In D, every unchanged shadow must have `d_i=3`, `q_i>=8`, and singleton capacity at least eight. The larger profiles require still higher capacities.

For C, the `d_v=2` profile is the exact six-singleton/three-pair support-two pattern from Section 9. All `d_v>=3` profiles force unchanged shadows of deficiency at least three; for example `(d_v,q)=(3,10)` forces `d_i=3`, `q_i>=9`, and singleton capacity at least seven.

These are the next finite targets. They can be attacked by extending the already committed six-row deficiency-three/high-capacity cover, rather than enumerating arbitrary eight-row states.
